/* data/learn-c/phy-11-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-08"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Optics is the study of light and its behaviour. This chapter covers reflection and refraction at plane and curved surfaces, total internal reflection, lenses, prisms and dispersion, some natural phenomena like the rainbow, defects of lenses, and the working of common optical instruments such as the microscope and the telescope."},"9.2":{"s":"Light is the radiation that lets us see. Newton thought it was a stream of particles; Huygens treated it as a wave. Today we know it has both wave and particle properties. Light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium, which is why ray optics works for objects much larger than its wavelength, while effects like interference and diffraction need the wave picture."},"9.3":{"s":"Ray optics treats light as rays that travel in straight lines. We use the Cartesian sign convention: distances are measured from the pole or optical centre, those along the incident light are positive and those against it negative, and heights above the principal axis are positive. A real object has a negative object distance; a real image from a mirror is also in front, so its distance is negative too.","w":["Apply the sign convention to every distance, including the focal length: the focal length of a concave mirror is negative."]},"9.4":{"s":"In reflection the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection. A plane mirror gives a virtual, upright, same-size image as far behind as the object is in front. Two mirrors at an angle θ form several images. A curved mirror has a focal length equal to half the radius of curvature, and its mirror formula links object distance, image distance and focal length. A convex mirror always gives a virtual image.","f":[{"x":"n = 360/theta","n":"Plane mirrors at angle theta: n = 360/theta. n even: N = n - 1. n odd: N = n - 1 (object on the bisector) or n (elsewhere). n not whole: N = whole part of n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.161"},{"x":"f = R/2","n":"Focal length of a spherical mirror; R = radius of curvature.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"},{"x":"1/f = 1/v + 1/u","n":"Mirror formula, with the Cartesian sign convention.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"},{"x":"P = 1/f","n":"Power of a mirror or lens in dioptre when f is in metres.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.4, p.162"}],"w":["Put u negative for a real object, and read the sign of v: negative means a real image in front of the mirror.","The formula holds for small apertures and rays close to the principal axis."]},"9.5":{"s":"Refraction is the bending of light when it passes from one medium to another, because its speed changes. The absolute refractive index of a medium is the speed of light in vacuum divided by its speed in the medium; a denser medium has a larger index. The relative index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 is the ratio of the indices. A pool looks shallower than it is.","f":[{"x":"n = c/v","n":"Absolute refractive index: c = speed of light in vacuum, v = speed in the medium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.164"},{"x":"n_21 = n_2/n_1 = v_1/v_2","n":"Refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.164"},{"x":"n = real depth/apparent depth","n":"Seen from above (nearly normal viewing) through a plane surface of a medium of index n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.5, p.165"}],"w":["The frequency of light does not change on refraction; speed and wavelength do.","Light bends towards the normal when it enters a denser medium."]},"9.6":{"s":"When light goes from a denser to a rarer medium, it bends away from the normal. At the critical angle of incidence the refracted ray grazes the surface at 90°. For larger angles the light is totally reflected back, which is total internal reflection. It needs light to travel from the denser to the rarer medium and the angle to exceed the critical angle. It is used in optical fibres, prism binoculars, periscopes and in the sparkle of diamonds.","f":[{"x":"sin(i_c) = 1/n","n":"Critical angle for light going from a medium of refractive index n into air.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.6, p.166"}],"w":["Total internal reflection is impossible from rarer to denser: the light must start in the denser medium.","A larger refractive index gives a smaller critical angle."]},"9.7":{"s":"Refraction at a spherical surface connects the object distance, image distance, radius of curvature and the two refractive indices. A thin lens is two such surfaces. Its focal length depends on the refractive index of the glass and the radii of curvature (lens maker's equation) and on the surrounding medium. Thin lenses in contact add their powers. A convex lens converges light and has positive focal length; a concave lens diverges it and has negative focal length.","f":[{"x":"n_2/v - n_1/u = (n_2 - n_1)/R","n":"Refraction at a single spherical surface: light goes from medium n_1 to medium n_2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.169"},{"x":"1/f = (n - 1)*(1/R_1 - 1/R_2)","n":"Lens maker's equation for a thin lens of index n in air.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.170"},{"x":"1/f = 1/v - 1/u","n":"Thin lens formula (the book's form, with the Cartesian sign convention).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.168"},{"x":"P = P_1 + P_2, 1/f = 1/f_1 + 1/f_2","n":"Two thin lenses in contact: powers add (in dioptre).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.168"},{"x":"f = R","n":"Symmetric double convex lens of index 1.5 (R_1 = R, R_2 = -R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.7, p.170"}],"w":["Use the sign convention for both radii: R₁ is positive and R₂ negative for a double convex lens.","Focal length of a lens changes with the medium: a lens of lower index than its surroundings acts the opposite way."]},"9.8":{"s":"A prism bends light by refraction at its two faces, and the ray deviates towards the base. The angle of deviation first falls and then rises as the angle of incidence increases; at minimum deviation the ray passes symmetrically. Different colours bend by different amounts, which splits white light into a spectrum: dispersion. Violet bends most and red least. A thin prism of small angle bends light by an angle proportional to (n − 1).","f":[{"x":"r_1 + r_2 = A","n":"Prism: A = angle of the prism, r_1 and r_2 = the angles of refraction at the two faces.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.172"},{"x":"i + e = A + delta","n":"Angle of deviation delta in terms of the angle of incidence i and emergence e.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.172"},{"x":"n = sin((A + delta_m)/2)/sin(A/2)","n":"Prism formula: the minimum deviation angle is written with a subscript m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.173"},{"x":"delta = A*(n - 1)","n":"Thin prism (A less than about 10 degrees).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.174"},{"x":"delta_V - delta_R = A*(n_V - n_R)","n":"Angular dispersion between violet and red.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.8, p.174"}],"w":["At minimum deviation i = e and r₁ = r₂ = A/2.","Violet has the larger refractive index and is deviated most."]},"9.9":{"s":"Sunlight produces several natural phenomena. A rainbow forms when sunlight is refracted, totally reflected inside raindrops and refracted again, spreading the colours. In the primary rainbow there is one internal reflection and red is on the outer edge; in the secondary rainbow there are two reflections and the order of colours is reversed. The Sun looks reddish at sunrise and sunset, and the sky looks blue, because of scattering of light by air molecules."},"9.10":{"s":"Real lenses and mirrors do not form perfect images. Spherical aberration occurs because rays far from the axis focus at a different point from rays near it; it is reduced by using a plano-convex lens with the curved face towards the incident rays, or a parabolic mirror. Chromatic aberration occurs because a lens bends each colour differently; it is reduced by an achromatic combination of a convex and a concave lens of different glasses.","w":["Chromatic aberration exists only for lenses (prisms disperse); mirrors do not show it because reflection does not depend on colour."]},"9.11":{"s":"Optical instruments make objects appear larger by increasing the angle they subtend at the eye. Magnifying power is the ratio of the angle subtended with the instrument to the angle subtended by the object at the least distance of distinct vision, D = 25 cm. A simple microscope is one convex lens. A compound microscope has an objective and an eyepiece, and its magnification is the product of the two. A telescope has a large objective and a small eyepiece.","f":[{"x":"M = beta/alpha = D/u","n":"Angular magnification of a simple microscope: D = 25 cm, u = object distance.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.181"},{"x":"M_max = 1 + D/f, M_min = D/f","n":"Simple microscope: image at the near point (D) gives the maximum, image at infinity the minimum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.181"},{"x":"M = m_o*M_e","n":"Compound microscope: lateral magnification of the objective times angular magnification of the eyepiece.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.182"},{"x":"M = f_o/f_e","n":"Astronomical telescope in normal adjustment (final image at infinity).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.183"},{"x":"L = f_o + f_e","n":"Length of the telescope in normal adjustment.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 9.11, p.183"}],"w":["Normal adjustment of a telescope means the final image is at infinity, so the lenses are f₀ + fₑ apart.","For a compound microscope, the objective has the SMALLER focal length of the two lenses."]}};
